        ":question/id": "7bb776d8-7980-55cf-9e30-dbe164ecec38",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate the instantaneous rate of change of the function $f(x)=-4tan⁡x$ when $x=\\pi .$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The instantaneous rate of change  when $x=\\pi $ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ at $x=\\pi .$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4tan⁡x) \\\\  & =-4{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\pi ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-4{sec}^{2}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the instantaneous rate of change is $-4.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
