        ":question/problem": "The derivative of $y=5x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =5-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =5-{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =5-(\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
