        ":question/id": "fe2456cb-8212-547a-8902-bba89e099b70",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate the instantaneous rate of change of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-6{x}^{2}$ when $x=0.$\n\n${f}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The instantaneous rate of change when $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-6{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x-12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(0)-12\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)})}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{1})}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change is $1.$",
